    "body": "### Problem\n\nDisclaimer: I was having so much trouble finding the culprit that I given AI the task to bisect each of my plugins to find what was causing this. tl;dr; I noticed when I was jumping between cursors with `[+C` and `]+C`, the cursors were being removed some times.\n\nWith noice.nvim attached as an external UI, any action that causes noice to render — an Ex command, a <Cmd> mapping, a Lua vim.cmd(), even typing : and aborting with <Esc> — silently deletes the multicursor located at the primary cursor's position. Other cursors are untouched. If the primary is not on a cursor, nothing is lost.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```lua\nlocal root = '/tmp/nvim-mc-repro'\nfor _, s in ipairs({\n    { 'nui.nvim', 'https://github.com/MunifTanjim/nui.nvim' },\n    { 'noice.nvim', 'https://github.com/folke/noice.nvim' },\n}) do\n    local path = root .. '/' .. s[1]\n    if vim.fn.isdirectory(path) == 0 then\n        vim.fn.system({ 'git', 'clone', '--depth=1', '--filter=blob:none', s[2], path })\n    end\n    vim.opt.runtimepath:prepend(path)\nend\nrequire('noice').setup({})\n\n```\n```\nnvim --clean -u repro.lua\nihello<CR>world<CR>third<Esc>\ngg  Q  j Q  j Q  gg          \" 3 cursors on lines 1-3, primary parked on cursor #1\n:echo 1<CR>                  \" or simply  :  then <Esc>\n```\n\nThe line-1 cursor is gone; lines 2 and 3 remain. gQ does not bring it back.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nI'd expect the cursor to not be removed.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nv0.13.0-nightly+4b3ab0d\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nonly nvim\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nNixOS 26.11\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nGhostty 1.3.1\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nNix (nixpkgs), neovim-nightly-overlay",
